The genealogy of Jesus.
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah was the father of Peres and Zarah. Tamar was their mother. Peres was the father of Hezron. Hezron was the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salma.
Salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of David the king. And David was the father of Solomon. And Solomon's mother was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Azariah was the father of Jotham, Jotham was the father of Ahaz, Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Heshaiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jehoiakim and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon.
After this, the family line continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. And Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azur was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Aaim. Aaim was the father of Elmodad.
Eliud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Mattathias. Mattathias was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were therefore fourteen generations from Abraham to David. And there were fourteen generations from David to the time of the Babylonian captivity of the Jews. And there were fourteen generations from that time to Christ. Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother was Mary, and Joseph was betrothed to her as husband. But before they came together, it was found that she was with child. He had conceived her with the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband Youssef was a religious man. He didn't want to expose her to the public. That's why he planned to divorce her as soon as possible.
But while Joseph was pondering this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. And the angel said to him, "Joseph son of David, do not fear to take Mary your wife into your home, for the child who is conceived in her is of the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet:
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son. She will call him Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God is with us."
Youssef woke up before the others. He did what he was ordered to do by the angel of the Lord. He took Mary to his house and made her his wife.
But he did not have relations with her until she gave birth to a son, and he named him Joseph Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea. This took place when Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
"Then they said to him, 'Where is the child who has been born to be king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the east and have come to worship him.'"
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed. And all the people in Jerusalem were also perplexed.
So Herod called the scribes and the chief priests from among the people and asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written through the prophet: 'And you, Bethlehem, in the land of Judah, are not the least among the rulers of Judah; for out of you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'"
"And you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come one who will be ruler over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5: 2)
Then Herod secretly called the Magi. He found out from them the exact time the star appeared.
He sent them to Bethlehem, saying, "Go and search diligently for the child. And when you find him, tell me so that I may also go and worship him."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they set out on their journey. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. It finally came to a stop over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their own country by another route. Jesus' family flees to Egypt
When the Magi had departed, Joseph saw a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. "Arise!" said the angel. "Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod will seek the child to destroy him."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. In this way, the words spoken by the Lord through the prophet were fulfilled. He had said, "I have decided to call my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding region. All the boy babies who were two years old or less were to be killed. This corresponded with the time when the wise men had seen the star.
In this way, the words of the prophet Jeremiah, which he had spoken, were fulfilled. He had said,
A voice is heard in Ramah, it is the voice of great weeping and sorrow. Rachel weeps for her children. She refuses to be comforted, because they are gone." (Jeremiah 31:15) The family of Jesus returns to Nazareth.
After Herod died, Joseph saw a dream while he was still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
Then Joseph arose, and took the Child and His mother Mary, and departed to the land of Israel.
But then he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. After he received a warning in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee instead.
Here he lived in a city called Nazareth. Thus, what the prophets had said about Jesus was fulfilled. They said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea, saying, "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand."
He said, "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said: "A voice cries out in the wilderness: 'Prepare the way of the Lord, make straight in the desert a highway for our God.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He wore a belt of skin around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people went out to him from Jerusalem and all Judea, and from all the region around the Jordan.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You are like a brood of vipers, the offspring of Satan. Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?"
Bring forth fruit worthy of repentance.
Do not suppose that you are saying, 'For Abraham the father,' and you will obtain this place of honor before God. For I say to you, From these stones God is able to raise up children to Abraham.
The shovel is still placed at the base of the tree. All fruit trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water and call upon you to repent of your sins. But after me will come one mightier than I, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and with fire."
From his hand the grain was scattered so that he could carry the straw from his threshing floor. He gathered his grain into the granary. But the chaff he would burn with unquenchable fire." John baptizes
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to stop him. Jesus said to him, "I need to be baptized by you. Why are you coming to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so now. It is right for us to do this. He is carrying out the holy plan of God." Then John agreed.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he went up out of the water. At that moment, heaven was opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my Son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Jesus is being tested.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. There, Satan tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus was hungry.
Then the devil came and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become loaves of bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He made him stand on the highest point of the temple.
"If you are the Son of God," he said, "throw yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11, 12)
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'Man shall not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
In the end, Satan took Jesus up to a very high mountain. He showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you bow down to me and worship me," he said, "I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Depart from me, Satan! For it is written: 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him alone you shall serve'" (Deuteronomy 6:13).
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus began to preach.
John the Baptist was imprisoned. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and resided in the city of Capernaum, which is by the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, the prophecy of the prophet Isaiah was fulfilled. He had said,
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jewish people live! The land along the Mediterranean Sea! The territory east of the Jordan River!
The people who dwell in darkness have seen a great light. They dwell in a land of deep darkness. But a light will shine on them." (Isaiah 9: 1,2)
From that time Jesus began to preach, saying, "Repent of your sins!" "The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples.
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were throwing a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow Me," said Jesus. "I will make you fish for people."
They scarcely heard it at all before they dropped their nets and followed him.
After that, he saw two other brothers. They were James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. And when they were preparing their nets, Jesus called them.
Immediately they left the boat, and their father, and followed Jesus. Jesus heals sick people.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news of God's kingdom. He healed all the people's diseases and suffering.
His news spread throughout all of Syria. People came to him bringing everyone who was sick with various diseases. Some were suffering greatly from severe pains. Others were possessed and controlled by demons. Some were shaking violently. Others were completely unable to move at all. Jesus healed them all.
Large crowds followed him. Some people came from Galilee, from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. Others came from the region located across the Jordan River.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up into a mountain, and when he had sat down, his disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said,
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer to do what is right. The celestial kingdom belongs to them.
Blessed are you when men revile you and persecute you with false accusations, because of me. Blessed are you, when men cast insults on you and tell all manner of evil against you because of me.
Be joyful and happy. Your reward in Paradise is great. In the same way, people harmed the prophets who lived a long time ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, but is thrown out and trampled underfoot.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket, but rather on a stand, so that those who come in can see the light.
Similarly, people don't turn on the light and put it under a glass. Instead, they put it in its base. Then, it gives light to everyone in the house.
In the same way, let your light shine before others. They will see the good things you do. And they will praise your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning of the law.
Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets. I have not come to abolish them, but to fulfill them.
What I am about to tell you is real. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not the smallest letter or stroke of the pen will disappear from the Law until everything is accomplished.
Do not disregard even the smallest commandment. And do not teach others to disregard them. If you do, you will be called the least important in the Kingdom of Heaven. Instead, practice and teach these commandments. Then you will be called important in the Kingdom of Heaven.
This is what I tell you: You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and the teachers of the law. If you are not, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. You have heard that it was said to the people long ago, 'You shall not murder; and anyone who murders will be subject to judgment.'
You have heard what was said to the people who lived a long time ago. It was said to them, "You shall not murder. (Exodus 20:13) Whoever murders will be judged for that."
But here is what I tell you: Do not become angry with your brother. Everyone who becomes angry with his brother will be subject to judgment. Again, whoever says to his brother, "Raca!" must be judged in the Sanhedrin. But whoever says, "You fool!" will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Let us suppose that you are offering your gift at the altar. And you remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift in front of the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother. Then come back and offer your gift.
Suppose someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Try to settle the matter quickly. Do so while you are still with him on your way. If you do not, he may hand you over to the judge. And the judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I will tell you is true. You will not leave until you pay the last cent! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't even look at a woman the wrong way. If anyone does that, he has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to lose one part of your body than for your whole body to be thrown into hell.
If your hand is sinning against you, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is nothing but a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your entire body enter hellfire. Divorce.
It was said, "When a man puts away his wife and sends her away, or when the woman leaves the man in this way, the man may not take her back to be his wife after she has been defiled. This is abhorrent to the Lord your God." (Deuteronomy 24: 1)
But here is what I say to you: Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. Perhaps a man may divorce his wife only if she has been unfaithful to him. Amen.
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break the covenants you make with the Lord. Keep the oaths you have sworn to Him."
Here is what I say to you. Never make such promises at all. Do not make them in heaven's name. That is God's throne.
Do not make them in the name of the earth. This is the place of the feet of God. Do not make them in the name of Jerusalem. This is the city of the great King.
And do not make your oaths a means of taking unjust gain among you, while keeping the fear of Allah before you. Thus, We clarify the verses for a people who understand. No, by your Lord, they will not believe until they make you a judge in the disputes that arise among them, and then find no resistance in their hearts to what you have decided and submit with full submission.
Just let "yes" mean "yes." Let "no" mean "no." Anything more than this comes from the devil. Be kind to others.
Have you heard that it was being said, "An eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth" (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But this is what I tell you: do not fight an evil person. "If someone strikes you on your right cheek, turn to him your left cheek as well."
If someone wants you to go to court with him for your shirt, let him take your coat as well.
Assume someone is forcing you to go one mile. Go with him two miles.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not turn away from someone who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
Have you heard them say, "Love your neighbor." (Leviticus 19:18) "Hate your enemy."
But here's what I tell you. Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
Then you will be sons of your Father who is in the heavens. For He causes His sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the just and the unjust.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even tax collectors do the same.
If you are only greeting your family, what else are you doing more than others? Even people who don't have religion do this.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving to those in need.
Beware of doing "good deeds" in front of others. Don't do them so that others can see you. If you do that, your Father in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to those in need, don't announce it by blowing trumpets. Don't be like those who pretend to be pious only for show. They announce what they do in temples and in the streets, desiring to be praised by others. But what I am about to tell you is true: they have received their full reward.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your charity will be secret, and your father will be the one who rewards you. He sees what you do secretly. Prayer
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites; for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and at the street corners, so that they may be seen by men. Truly, I say to you, they have received their reward in full.
When you pray, go into your room, and close the door and pray to your Father who is in secret, and your Father who sees the things done in secret will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret.
When you pray, do not babble on as the evil people do. They think that they will be answered because they babble on so much.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
This is how you should pray: "Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name."
Your dominion is achieved. What you want to happen on earth is done as it is in heaven.
Give us our daily bread for this day.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Protect us from falling into sin when tempted. Deliver us from evil.'
Forgive others when they wrong you. If you do, your Father who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
When you walk without eating, you do not look as gloomy as those who only pretend to be pious. They make their faces very sad. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I am about to tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
But when you go without eating, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then the others will not know that you are fasting. Only your unseen Father will know this. And He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret. Store up your treasures in heaven.
Do not accumulate for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
Instead of that, save up for yourselves treasures in heaven. For there, moth and rust do not corrupt, and thieves do not break in and steal.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. Suppose your eyes are good. Then your whole body will be filled with light.
But suppose your eyes are bad. Then your whole body will be filled with darkness. If the light within you is darkness, it is the blackest darkness!
There is no one who cannot serve two masters at the same time. He will hate one and love the other, or he will be a slave to one and wanting to be away from the other. It is not possible to serve God and money at the same time. Do not worry.
Let me tell you, do not be afraid. Do not be afraid for your life, and for what you will eat and drink. And do not be afraid for your body, and for what you will wear. Is there not more to life than eating? Are there not things more important for the body than clothing?
Look at the birds of the sky, for they neither sow nor reap nor gather into barns; yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much better than they?
Can you add even an hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you worry about clothing? Look at how flowers grow in nature. They do nothing and make no clothing.
But here is what I say to you. Even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of those flowers.
If God clothes the wild grass this way, will He not clothe you even better? After all, the grass is only here today. Tomorrow it is thrown into the fire. Your faith is very small indeed!
So, do not be afraid. Do not say, "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
People without religion run after all these things. Your father in heaven knows that you need these things.
But seek first the kingdom of God. Do His will. Then all these things will be given to you as well.
So don't worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day has its own troubles.
Be fair in your judgment of others.
Do not judge others, and you will not be judged.
You will judge yourself by the same standard by which you judge others. You will be measured by the same measure you measure others.
You see the speck in your friend's eye, but you don't notice the log in your own eye.
How can you tell your friend, "Let me take out some of the sawdust from your eyes"? And how can you say this when you have a piece of wood in your eye?
Hypocrite! First, take the plank out of your own eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to take the speck of dust out of your friend's eye.
Do not give what is holy to dogs, and do not throw your pearls before swine, or they may trample them under their feet, and then turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask, and it will be given to you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and the door will be opened to you.
Everyone who asks will receive. Whoever seeks will find. The door will be opened for the one who knocks.
Hypothetically, if your children asked you for bread, who among you would give them a stone?
Or suppose he asks for a fish. Which of you would give him a snake?
Even if you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father who is in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what was written in the law and in the prophets. The great and small doors
Enter the Kingdom of God through the narrow door. For wide is the gate and broad is the road that leads to destruction, and many are those who enter through it.
But the gate is narrow and the road that leads to life is also narrow. Few people find it. The tree and its fruits.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You will know them by their deeds. "Do people pick grapes from thorny bushes? Do they pick figs from thorny thickets?
In the same way, every good tree produces good fruit. But a bad tree produces bad fruit.
A good tree does not bear bad fruit, and a bad tree does not bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down. It is thrown into the fire.
You can know a tree by its fruits.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven will enter.
Many will say to me on that day: 'Lord, Lord, is not this the name by which we prophesied, cast out demons, and performed many miracles in your name?'
Then I tell them plainly, "I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!" The builders, the wise and the foolish.
Therefore, everyone who hears my words and acts on them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The rain is falling. The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. But it is not falling down. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.
The rain is falling. The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. And it falls with a loud, high-pitched sound."
Jesus continued to speak all these things. The crowd was amazed at his teaching.
He taught as one having authority, and not as their scribes.
Jesus heals a man who had leprosy.
Jesus came down from the mountain. Large crowds followed him.
A man who had a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus, saying, "Lord, if you are willing, you can make me clean."
Messiah reached out his hand and touched the man. "I am ready to do it," he said. "Be 'clean'!" Suddenly, the man was healed of his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." A Roman centurion believed.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him and asked for his help.
"Sir," he said, "my servant is lying in the house and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Sir, I am not worthy to have you enter my house. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
I am Rasy Adi Qaid, and I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to one: "Go", so he goes. I say to another: "Come", so he comes. And I say to my servant: "Do this thing", so he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. And he said to the disciples who were following him, "What I am about to tell you is true. In Israel, I have not found anyone whose faith is as strong as this."
As I tell you, many will come from the East and the West, and will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. They will sit down with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But those who believe they belong to the kingdom will be thrown out, into the darkness. There they will weep and grind their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman leader, "Go! It will be as you believed." And at that very hour, his servant was healed. Jesus heals many people.
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hands, and the fever left her. She vomited and began to serve him.
When evening came, many who were possessed by demons were brought to Jesus. And he drove out the spirits by a word of command. And he healed all the sick.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said. He said, "He was wounded for our transgressions, and He was bruised for our iniquities. The chastisement for our peace was upon Him, and by His stripes we are healed." (Isaiah 53:4-5) Following Jesus costs
Jesus saw the crowds surrounding him. Then he told his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then one of the scribes came to him and said to him, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have holes and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
Another disciple said to him, "Teacher, permit me to go and bury my father first."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus calms the storm.
Jesus got into a boat, and his disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a terrible storm came upon the lake. The waves were beating the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
His disciples went to him and woke him up. They said to him, "Master! Save us! We are going to perish!"
He answered them, "You of little faith! Why are you so afraid?" And He got up and rebuked the winds and the sea, and there was a great calm.
The students were surprised. They asked, "What kind of man is this? Even the winds and waves obey him!" Jesus heals two men who are demon-possessed.
Jesus arrived on the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. He encountered two men who were possessed by demons. They had come from the tombs. These men were so incredibly savage that no one could pass by that way.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you come here to punish us before the time of reckoning comes?"
Not far away, there was a large herd of pigs feeding.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, "If you drive us out, send us to the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. The herd of pigs, about two thousand in number, rushed down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.
Those who were tending the pigs fled. They went to a city and told everything. They said to the people, "What has happened to the men who had the demons cast out of them?"
Then all the city went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to depart from their region.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus entered a boat. He went to the other side of the lake and came to his own town.
Some men brought a man who couldn't walk, lying on a mat. Jesus saw their faith. That's why the man told him, "Don't lose hope, my son. Your sins are forgiven."
Then some of the Pharisees in the crowd said to one another, "This man is blaspheming!"
