The man's enemies will be his own family members. (Micah 7:6)
Anyone who loves their father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. Anyone who loves their son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And everyone who does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
If anyone finds his life, he will lose it. If anyone loses his life for me, he will find it.
Whoever receives you receives me, and whoever receives me receives the one who sent me.
Presumptively, if a person receives a prophet as a prophet, he will receive the reward of a prophet. And if a person receives a righteous person as a righteous person, he will receive the reward of the righteous.
Hypothetically, if someone even gave a glass of cold water to a little child who is following me, the story I am going to tell you is true. He will be compensated for sure.
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples. After that, he went to teach and preach in the villages of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who is coming, or should we wait for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go back to John and tell him what you hear and see."
The blind can see. The disabled can walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf can hear. The dead are resurrected. Good news is brought to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not abandon their faith for me.
While John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John, saying, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
If it is not so, what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. People who dress in fine clothes are found in the palaces of kings.
Then what did you go out into the desert to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one of whom it is written in the book, "Behold, I send My angel before Your face, who will prepare Your way before You." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am about to tell you is true. No one more important than John the Baptist has ever been born. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is more important than he is.
For John the Baptist's days, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing. And powerful people seize it.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept that, John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
He who has ears, let him hear.
How shall I liken the people of today? Like children, sitting in the markets, calling to themselves, saying:
