"My service is easy and my burden is light."
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath, Jesus walked through the grainfields. And his disciples were hungry, so they began to pick the heads of grain and eat.
The Pharisees saw this; and they said to Jesus, "Look! This is against the Law to do on the Sabbath. But your disciples are doing it anyway!"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read what David and his men did?
He entered the house of God, he and his men, and they ate the bread of sanctity. It was not permitted to eat it except for the priests.
Have you not read the law? For it tells how the priests in the temple are to do their work each Sabbath day. But they are not considered guilty.
We tell you that something more important than the temple exists here.
The inscription says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not accuse the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
So then, Jesus came to that place, and he entered their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and deformed hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they said to him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath, or not?"
He said to them: "What would you do if one of your sheep fell into a well on the Sabbath? Would you not hold onto it and pull it out?"
A man is worth more than sheep! So the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out, and it was restored, like the other.
But the Pharisees went out and plotted how they might kill Jesus, the chosen servant of God.
Jesus was aware of all the plans of the Pharisees. So, He left that place. Many followed Him, and He healed all their sick.
But warn them not to tell who it is.
This was to fulfill what was spoken through the prophet Isaiah, who said,
