Jesus answered him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. And he said, "Behold my mother! Behold my brothers!"
Everyone who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
That very day, Jesus left the house and sat by the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd gathered around him. So he entered a boat. And he sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things through stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds."
He scattered the seeds on the ground. Some of them fell on a path. Birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun came out, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Some seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and overcame the plants.
Other remaining seeds fell on fertile ground, grew, and yielded 100, 60, or 30 times more than the seeds that were sown.
He who has ears should listen.
His disciples came to him, and asked, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered, "You have been given an opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. This has not been given to foreigners."
Everyone who possesses this type of knowledge will be given more. In fact, they will have a very great deal. If no one has this type of knowledge, even what little they have will be taken away from them.
Here is why I use parables when I speak to people. I say, "They see, but they don't really see. They listen, but they don't really hear or understand."
In them the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled, when he said: "You will hear and not understand, and see and not perceive."
The hearts of this people have become dull, and their ears are hard of hearing, and their eyes haveغلق, lest they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and return and I should heal them.' (Isaiah 6: 9, 10)
But how blessed are your eyes because they see, and your ears because they hear.
What I will tell you is true. Many prophets and righteous people wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the meaning of the farmer's story.
People hear the message about the kingdom but do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was sown in their hearts. These people are like the seed that was sown on a road.
Others received the seed that fell into rocky places. These are the ones who hear the message and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. Therefore, they last only for a little while. They quickly fall away from faith when temptation or suffering comes because of the message.
Some of them received the seed that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the message. But afterward, they are crowded out by the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth. They prevent them from producing fruit.
But others received the seed that fell on good soil. They are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop that is 100, 60, or 30 times more than what the farmer planted. "The Parable of the Weeds"
Jesus spoke another parable to the people: "The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
His slaves, the owner of the land, came to him and said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? From where then have these weeds sprung up?"
"My enemy is the one who did this," he answered. The servants asked him, "Shall we go and pull out those weeds?"
"No," the owner replied. "While you are pulling out the weeds, you might pull out the wheat along with it."
Let them grow together until the harvest. Then I will tell the workers what to do. Here is what I will tell them: "First, gather the weeds. Tie them into bundles to burn. Then gather the wheat. Bring it to my storage." The parables of the mustard seed and the yeast
Jesus said to the crowd another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that a man took and planted in his field.
It is the smallest of your seeds, yet when it is grown it is the largest of the trees of the garden. It becomes a great tree, so that the birds of the sky come and nest in its branches.
Jesus said to them, "The Kingdom of Heaven is like a woman who took yeast and hid it in three measures of flour until all of it was leavened."
Jesus used to tell all these things to the people through parables. He did not tell them anything without telling them a story.
Jesus explains the parable of the weeds.
Then Jesus went out from the midst of the people, and went into the house. And his disciples came to him, saying, "Explain to us the parable of the tares of the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man.
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the devil.
The enemy who sowed them is the devil. The harvest is the day of judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are uprooted and burned in the fire. Thus it will be on the day of judgment.
The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will gather out of his kingdom all causes of sin and those who do iniquity. And they will cast them into the furnace of fire; there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
They will throw them into the fiery furnace. There the people will cry and gnash their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in their father's kingdom. Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear. Tales of the hidden treasure and the pearl
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, and out of joy went and sold all that he had, and bought that field.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant in search of fine pearls.
He found a very valuable one. So he went and sold everything he had. And he bought that pearl. The Parable of the Net.
Again, Jesus spoke to the crowds in parables: "The kingdom of heaven is like a net that was thrown into the lake. When it was full of fish, they dragged the net ashore, sat down, and put the good fish into baskets. They threw the bad fish away."
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they threw the bad fish away.
This is how the Day of Judgment will be. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
The wicked people will be thrown into the fiery furnace. There the wicked will weep and grind their teeth.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He said to them: "Every scholar of the Law who has learned about the Kingdom of the heavens is like a man who brings out of his treasure what is new and what is old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus continued to tell these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began teaching people in their synagogues. They were amazed and said, "Where did this man get this wisdom? And where did he get these miraculous powers?"
Is not this the son of the carpenter? Is not his mother's name Mary? Are not his brothers James, John, Judas, and Simon?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They didn't like him at all. But Issa told them: "A prophet is not respected in his own country. And he does not receive any respect in his own home."
