John the Baptist's head is cut off.
In those days, Herod the ruler was on his throne in Galilee, and the two regions of the Jordan were hearing about the wonders that Jesus was performing.
He said to his retinue, "This is John the Baptist. He has arisen from among the dead! Therefore, he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him, and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Philip, the brother of Herod.
John was telling Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On the day of Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced before Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
So he promised her by an oath to give her whatever she asked for.
The mother said to her daughter, "What are you saying?" And the girl said to Herod, "I want the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very angry. But he thought of his promise and of his dinner guests. So he asked one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
They brought his head on a large platter and gave it to the girl. She now carried it and took it to her wife.
The disciples of John came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowd heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
In the evening, His disciples approached Him and said, "The place is deserted, and the hour is already late. Send the crowd away to the villages and farms around, so they can buy food for themselves."
Jesus answered, "There is no need for them to go away. You give them something to eat."
We only have five loaves of bread and two fish, he replied.
He said, "Bring them here to me."
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. He broke the loaves into pieces. Then He gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. The disciples gathered twelve baskets full of leftover bread.
About 5000 men who ate. Even women and children ate. Jesus walks on water.
Immediately Jesus entered the boat with the disciples, and He ordered them to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. And after that, He dismissed the crowd.
After they left, he went and climbed the mountain to pray alone. Night came and he was still alone.
The boat was very far from the shore. The waves were hitting it because the wind was blowing against it.
In the early morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and drew back in terror. "It's a ghost!" they said. And they screamed in fear.
Jesus immediately called out to them and said, "Be courageous! I am here. Do not be afraid."
"Sir, are you the one?" Peter asked. "If it is you, command me to come to you on the water."
"Come," said Jesus. So Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. And beginning to sink, he cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him, and said to him, "O you of little faith, why did you doubt?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
And those in the boat worshiped Jesus and said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and reached Gennesaret.
The men who lived there knew Jesus. So they sent a message into all the surrounding areas. People brought all their sick to Jesus.
They begged him to allow the sick just to touch the hem of his garment. And all who touched him were healed.
Why do people call others "filthy" for doing what everyone else does?
The Pharisees and the scribes from Jerusalem came to Jesus to see who he was. And they asked him,
Why are your students disobedient and not informed by adults? Your students don't wash their hands before eating!
Jesus answered: "And why do you not obey the command of God? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother" (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16). And He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother shall be put to death" (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9).
But you allow people to tell their parents: "Any help you may have received from us is a gift intended for God."
They do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You nullify the word of God to follow the teachings of your religion.
O imposters! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:
These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship of Him does not benefit me in the least. Indeed, they only do what You command them to do, themselves." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowds to Him. And He said, "Listen and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." It is what comes out of your mouth that makes you "unclean."
Then the disciples came to Him, saying, "Do You know that the Pharisees were deeply offended after hearing this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants my Father in heaven did not plant; these will be pulled up by the roots.
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit."
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Do you still not understand?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach. And then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. These things are what make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts proceed from the heart. So do murder, adultery, sexual immorality, theft, false testimony, and slander.
Those are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
There was a woman of the Canaanites who resided in those regions. She came to him and cried out, saying, "Lord, Son of David! Have mercy on me! My daughter is afflicted by a demon that torments her greatly."
Jesus did not utter a single word. His disciples approached him and implored him, saying, "Remove her, because she is crying out behind us!"
Jesus answered, "I was not sent except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Master! Help me!" she said.
He answered, "It is not right to take the bread from your children's mouths and give it to the dogs."
"Yes, sir," she said. "But even dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."
Then Jesus answered and said to her, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be to you as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that hour. Jesus feeds the four thousand.
Jesus left there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to a mountain and sat down.
They brought him in great multitudes. They also brought those who were blind and lame. They also brought the crippled, those who could not speak, and many others. And they laid them at his feet, and he healed them.
And the people marveled. Those who were speechless spoke, the lame walked, the lepers were cleansed, and the blind saw. And the people glorified the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said to them, "I have compassion for these people. They have already been with me for three days, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, for if I did, they would become too weak on their journey home."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here," and they said, "Where will we find enough bread to satisfy these vast crowds?"
"How many loaves do you have?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they replied, "and a few small fish."
Jesus told the crowd to sit on the ground.
Taking the seven loaves and the fish, He thanked God, broke them, and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples distributed them to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples collected seven baskets of leftovers.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Even women and children ate.
After the audience enjoyed themselves with Issa, he boarded the ferry. He went to the area close to Majdan.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
Answer: "At night you look at the sky. You say: It will be fine weather. The sky is red."
And in the morning she says, "Today will be windy. The sky is red and cloudy." She knows what she sees in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
A wicked and unbelieving people seek a sign from heaven. And no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Jesus' disciples crossed over to the other side of the lake. They forgot to bring bread.
"Beware," Jesus said to them, "of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students talked about this among themselves themselves. They said: "He says this because we didn't bring bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. That's why he said to them, "Your faith is very weak! Why are you discussing among yourselves the lack of bread?"
Don't you understand yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets of fragments you picked up?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets full of pieces of bread you picked up?
How is it possible that you do not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was not advising them to beware of the yeast used in bread. He was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees taught. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus walked to the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say is the Son of Man?"
They answered, "Some people say he is John the Baptist. Others say he is Elijah. And others say he is Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood have not revealed this to you, but the Father who is in heaven."
This is what I say to you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys to paradise. What you close on earth will be closed in heaven. What you open on earth will be opened in heaven."
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks of his coming death.
From that hour, Jesus began to explain to His disciples what was going to happen to Him. He told them that He had to go to Jerusalem. There, He had to suffer many things at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the scribes. He had to be killed and on the third day, rise again.
Taking Jesus aside, Peter began to rebuke him, saying, "God forbid, Lord! This will never happen to you!"
Jesus melted and said to Peter: "Get out of my way, Satan! You are obstructing me. You are not concerned about what is God's, but about what is people's."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, he must say no to himself. He must take up his cross and follow me."
If anyone wants to save their life, they will lose it. But if anyone loses their life for me, they will find it.
What benefit would it be if someone gained the whole world but lost their own soul? Or what could someone possibly give in exchange for their own soul?
Indeed, the son of man is about to come in the glory of his father, with his angels, and then he will reward each one according to his deeds.
What I am about to tell you is real. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom.
The Transfiguration of Jesus
After six days, Jesus took with him Peter and James and John, the brother of James. He brought them up onto a high mountain. There they were all alone.
Here before them his appearance was transformed. His face shone like the sun. His clothing became white as light.
In that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three dwellings. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice from the cloud said, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased. Listen to him."
When the disciples heard this, they were terrified and fell to the ground with their faces down.
But Jesus came and touched them. "Arise," he said. "Do not be afraid."
When they raised their heads, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And on the way, Jesus told them what they must do. "Do not tell anyone," he said to them, "what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man is raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the legal scholars say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "Yes, Elijah is supposed to come and make everything new again."
But I tell you: Elijah has come, and they did not recognize him, and they did to him whatever they wanted. So also the Son of Man will suffer at their hands.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As they came near the crowd, a man approached Jesus; he knelt before him.
"Sir," he said, "have mercy on my son. He is trembling violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought it to you among your students. But they couldn't cure it."
"O ye infidels and ye evildoers!" Jesus answered. "How long shall I yet be with you? How long shall I yet suffer you? Bring the child hith unto me."
Jesus commanded the demon to leave the child, and it departed from him. He was healed at that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. They asked him, "Why could we not drive out that demon?"
He answered, "Because your faith is too small. What I am about to tell you is true. If you have faith the size of a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
Translation
They gathered in Galilee, and Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man is to be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead." The disciples were filled with deep sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax.
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him: "Doesn't your teacher pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does that," he answered. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "On whom do the kings of the earth collect taxes and fees? Do they collect it from their sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children need not pay," Jesus said to him.
But we don't want to anger them. Go to the lake and cast your line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. You will find there the exact coin you need. Take it and give it to them as a tax for me and for you.
